#Bugs, Debugging and Benchmarking

## It worked. And then it didn't.

The capacity aware simulation was finally doing what I wanted.

Elevators could have routes, pick up additional passengers, and make better use of their available capacity.

Then I left it running for a while.

At first, everything looked fine.

And then the elevators started freezing.

Not immediately. Not in an obvious way.

The simulation could run for a few rounds, passengers would move around, and then eventually the cars would stop responding properly.

Waiting passengers would remain on the floors while empty elevators sat around doing nothing useful.

This was the kind of failure I wanted the Playground to produce.

Not because bugs are fun, but because a simulation that runs for thirty seconds isn't very interesting.

A simulation that runs for long enough to expose assumptions is.

---

## Why I could actually investigate it

The important part was that I wasn't approaching the failure as a black box.

By this point, I had spent a lot of time reading through the implementation and sketching how the modules interacted.

I knew exactly where:

* elevator movement was handled
* requests were created
* dispatch decisions were made
* routes were represented
* passenger state changed
* simulation events were processed
* statistics were collected

So when I saw an elevator freeze, I could reason backwards from the behavior.

If the elevator was physically capable of moving but wasn't receiving a useful destination, that pointed toward routing or assignment.

If passengers appeared to be assigned but never boarded, I could look at the interaction between request state, route execution, and passenger handling.

The failure itself gave me a clue about which layer was probably responsible.

That was one of the clearest benefits of the previous stage.

Understanding the architecture made debugging much faster.

I didn't need to inspect every line of the project every time something went wrong.

I could start with the part of the system whose responsibilities matched the symptom.

---

## Start with the symptom, not the fix

My first hypothesis was that the capacity aware dispatcher was producing bad assignments.

That seemed reasonable.

The new behavior was the thing that had just been added, and the failure appeared after introducing multi passenger routing.

Maybe an elevator was being marked as full when it wasn't.

Maybe a request was being assigned to an elevator that could no longer reach it.

Maybe the route was becoming inconsistent.

Instead of immediately changing the dispatcher, I wanted to reproduce the problem and narrow down where the failure actually happened.

---

## Reproducing the failure

The first useful step was reducing the problem.

Rather than trying to understand everything happening in a busy simulation, I wanted a smaller, deterministic scenario that could produce the same failure.

Reproducibility mattered here.

If the simulation uses random passenger traffic, a bug can appear once and then disappear on the next run simply because the passengers arrived differently.

That makes debugging much harder.

Using controlled seeds meant I could say:

> This sequence of passengers causes the failure.

Then I could run that same sequence again after making a change.

That made the debugging process much more like an experiment.

same initial conditions
        ↓
same traffic
        ↓
same failure
        ↓
change one thing
        ↓
run again
        ↓
compare

Without reproducibility, it is difficult to know whether a fix actually fixed the problem or whether the randomness behind it simply changed.

---
## From debugging to benchmarking

Once the obvious failures were fixed, the next question became more interesting.

It was no longer:

Does the simulation work?

It became:

What does each strategy actually do under different conditions?

That is where benchmarking comes in.

I don't want to compare two dispatchers using one random run and declare one of them better.

The environment itself can change dramatically depending on the traffic.

A quiet building is a very different problem from a building where passengers are constantly arriving.

So I want to eventually run controlled experiments across different traffic conditions.

For example:

arrival rate
    ↓
low / high

traffic pattern
    ↓
normal / concentrated / rush hour / directional

dispatcher
    ↓
baseline / capacity aware / future strategies

Then I can observe how performance changes.

## Reproducibility is part of fairness

The same principle that helped debug the bugs becomes important for benchmarking.

If I compare two strategies, they should experience the same conditions.

The dispatcher should be the variable that changes.

This doesn't make an experiment perfectly representative of the real world.

But it makes the comparison much more meaningful.

Instead of asking:

"Which run looked better?"

I can ask:

"When both strategies faced the same conditions, how did their behavior differ?"

What I actually want to measure

There isn't one number that completely describes an elevator system.

So the benchmark needs to look at several dimensions.

A strategy might work extremely well under light traffic but degrade sharply once demand increases.

Another might maintain reasonable throughput but cause certain passengers to wait much longer.

Those differences are much more useful to study than simply saying that one dispatcher is better.

Understanding constraints, not chasing one winner

This is also where I want the project to go beyond comparing algorithms.

Different strategies impose different constraints.

A strategy that prioritizes efficiency might create longer waits for certain passengers.

A strategy that aggressively groups passengers might reduce the number of trips but increase route complexity.

A strategy that tries to serve everyone immediately might create unnecessary movement.

A strategy that waits for better grouping opportunities might improve overall efficiency while making some individual passengers wait longer.

I don't want the simulation to simply produce a leaderboard.

I want it to help reveal what each strategy gives up in order to achieve something else.

That is a much more interesting question to me.

## Towards more realistic scenarios

The current traffic generator is intentionally simple.

That's useful for getting the system working, but real buildings don't produce perfectly uniform random passenger traffic.

Eventually I want to introduce controlled scenarios such as:

low traffic
normal traffic
rush hour
morning upward traffic
evening downward traffic
concentrated floor demand
mixed directional traffic
increasingly overloaded conditions

The purpose isn't to perfectly simulate every real building.

It's to create different environments in which the assumptions of a strategy become visible.

If a strategy performs well under one traffic pattern and poorly under another, that's useful information.

It tells me something about the conditions under which the strategy makes sense.

## Where RL might eventually fit

I still don't have a final RL formulation for this.

And I'm okay with that.

The more I work on the simulation, the more I think that forcing an RL agent into it too early would have missed the interesting part.

Right now I have a world in which decisions matter.

Only after understanding that space better does it make sense to ask whether a learned policy could discover something that my manually designed strategies cannot.

The eventual RL problem might involve deciding which requests to serve, how to construct routes, when to redirect an elevator, or some combination of these.

But I don't need to decide that yet.

The environment is becoming useful before the agent exists.

## The bigger direction

I don't want the elevator simulation to end with:

"I built an elevator dispatcher."

I want it to become a way of reasoning about a real world system.

The interesting question isn't simply how to make elevators move faster.

It's how different ways of making decisions change the behavior of the entire system.

## Why the Playground exists

This is also why the debugging process mattered so much.

The elevator freezing was frustrating, but it was also evidence that the project had reached the point where I could actually investigate a system rather than just write code.

I had an idea.

I turned it into a world.

I built a strategy.

The strategy broke the world.

I traced the failure back through the system, fixed it, and then started asking how to measure the behavior properly.

The goal isn't to find a perfect answer immediately.

It's to build systems that make difficult questions easier to see and reason about.

And eventually, I want the elevator simulation to get close to that point:

a place where different strategies can be tested under meaningful constraints;

where their tradeoffs become visible, and where the simulation can help someone reason about how better solutions might actually be designed.

That is a much bigger goal than the idea I started with.
